title: ElegyDialogue
---


<<if DoesHeKnow("ElegySetting")>>	
	(Enter ELEGY)
<<else>>
	<<Learn ElegySetting>>
	SETTING - In the stark shadow of a golden building in Nauka Square.

	AT RISE - In front of SASHA stands a tall and powerful figure, watching beneath an overhang before an entrance.

	NARRATOR: The radiant Elegy. It's impossible to not be starstruck in her presence. 
	NARRATOR: The immortal who established the faith in Solster. A faith so interwoven into its culture that they are practically one in the same. 
	NARRATOR: Elegy, a living idol of the church of Solster, and relevant to mention, your brother's boss.
	<< Learn ElegyTarot >>
	<< Learn ElegyInfoA >> //The founder of the church of Solster
<<endif>>


Elegy: Welcome home, Sasha Yıldız.
Elegy: They say death brings reunion. Evidently, it is true that it brings people together again.
Elegy: Have you spoken to your brother?

-> “Yeah, I've spoken to him…” <<if DoesHeKnow("DmitriyTarot")>>
    Sasha: “Yeah, I've spoken to him…”
	Elegy: Was it not the welcome you expected?
	Elegy: I apologise on his behalf. I am partly to blame, with how busy I have kept him as of late. 
	Elegy: But he has been a great help in our preparations for our festival. Once it is done, you shall have the time together that you desire.
-> “Not yet.”
    Sasha: “Not yet.”
	Elegy: If you wish to, you can find him nearby. He is currently taking a break, I believe.
	She turns her head as if to seek out where Dmitriy is. Her neck seems to barely turn. She doesn’t try very hard.
	Elegy: I apologise for having kept him so busy. He has been a great help in our preparations for our festival. 
    Elegy: Once it is done, you shall have the time together that you desire.

Elegy: The Parade this year shall be quite special, you see.
Elegy: It needs to be perfect. That is an imperative. 
Elegy: Wouldn't you agree, Sasha? Considering our beloved Alfonz will participate this year as well.
NARRATOR: Alfonz… Hearing the name spoken by her sends a wave of shock over you. As if now his death has been declared official.
NARRATOR: You still can't believe he’s really gone. That you will never hear one of his lectures again. That you won’t hear his soft humming when he believes you to be asleep. 
NARRATOR: Elegy is right. No matter what, you won’t ever be able to forget this year’s Parade.
Elegy: You shall attend the parade today, correct.

-> “Yes, I will.”
    Sasha: “Yes, I will.”
	Elegy: (smiling?) Good.
-> “Obviously, duh.”
    Sasha: “Obviously, duh.”
	Elegy: (smiling?) Good. 
-> “Do I really have a choice?”
    Sasha: “Do I really have a choice?”
	Elegy: I suppose not. Not anymore.
    << if $ArtMajor == true >>
        There is a hidden meaning behind what she just said. One that you don’t have the means of knowing yet.
	    The dramatic and narrative weight behind it is still palpable, even if you don’t understand why.
    << endif >>
	
<< if $PsychMajor == true >>
	She is used to getting her way. Inflexible, self-centered… If she were human, you could diagnose her with delusions of grandeur.
	Mortal psychology might not apply well to an immortal, though. Her grandeur is well justified.
<< elseif $TechMajor == true >>
	What kind of logic gate is this? One where every input results in ‘True’? What is the use for that?
	You have never understood why people ask questions when they will only accept one answer. In that sense, you welcome Elegy's lack of pretense. 
<< endif >>

She extends her hand, enticing you to move closer.


-> Take her hand 
    Sasha: (Take her hand)
    It feels like she could crush your hand if she wished to, with just a squeeze. Instead however, she withdraws and turns away.
	NARRATOR: As quickly as she appeared, she disappears again. Presumably she is back to wandering Solster as she often does, or she is making sure everything is in order for her perfect parade.
-> Do nothing 
    Sasha: (Do nothing.)
	Elegy: (after a few seconds of silence, pulling back hand) Very well.
    NARRATOR: As quickly as she appeared, she disappears again. Presumably she is back to wandering Solster as she often does, or she is making sure everything is in order for her perfect parade.
===